---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { EMAIL, LINKS } from '../data/six10nine';
---

<Base title="Security" description="How to report a vulnerability in six10nine." docs>
  <p class="eyebrow">Docs</p>
  <h1>Security</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    six10nine sees key presses, clicks menu items in other apps through Accessibility, and runs a bundled BOINC client,
    so security reports matter.
  </p>

  <h2>Report a vulnerability</h2>
  <p>
    Email <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL.security}`}>{EMAIL.security}</a>. Please don’t open a public issue. Include:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>what’s affected: the version, macOS version, and Apple silicon or Intel;</li>
    <li>how to reproduce it, and what an attacker could do;</li>
    <li>whether you’d like credit, and under what name.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We aim to acknowledge reports within 3 business days and to keep you updated until there’s a fix. Please give us
    reasonable time to release a fix before disclosing publicly; we’ll agree a date with you.
  </p>

  <h2>Bug bounty</h2>
  <p>Not live yet, and there are no paid rewards yet. Until a programme launches, report by email.</p>

  <h2>Scope</h2>
  <p>In scope:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>the six10nine app (<code>Six10Nine.app</code>) and its bundled <code>six10nine-mcp</code> server;</li>
    <li>the installers: <code>install.sh</code>, the build scripts and the Homebrew cask;</li>
    <li>how six10nine configures, starts and talks to the bundled BOINC client (<code>127.0.0.1:31418</code>).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Out of scope: vulnerabilities in BOINC itself or in Einstein@home’s servers (report those to
    <a href={LINKS.boinc}>BOINC</a> and <a href={LINKS.einstein}>Einstein@home</a>), in macOS, or in AI clients that run
    <code>six10nine-mcp</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Supported versions</h2>
  <p>Only the latest release gets security fixes.</p>

  <h2>Good to know</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>six10nine-mcp</code> only speaks over stdio to the process that started it, and can only read and change
      six10nine’s chord settings. Treat an MCP client that runs it as trusted to change your chords.
    </li>
    <li>
      The BOINC client runs with <code>--insecure</code>, so Einstein@home’s science apps run as your user rather than in
      BOINC’s separate sandbox user. Setting up that sandbox needs an administrator password, as BOINC’s own installer
      does.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="footnote"><a href="/.well-known/security.txt">security.txt</a></p>
</Base>
